            <ArticleTitle>[Clinical observation of tramadol on postoperative painment from LASEK].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>114-5, 123</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate the efficacy and safety of tramadol for controlling post-operative ocular pain from laser assisted subepithelial keratomileusis (LASEK).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A randomized and controlled study was used. Tramadol tablet is taken orally two times a day plus Tobradex and Diclofenac sodium eyedrops were administered four times a day to the study group and Tobradex and Diclofenac sodium eyedrops only were administered four times a day to the control group. Slitlamp biomicroscopy and clinical scoring (0 to 4) were used to assess pain, photophobia, tearing, stinging-burning (0 = no pain; 1 = mild pain; 2 = moderate pain; 3 = severes pain; 4 = very severe pain). The assessments were made at anterioroperation, 1 day, 4 days,7 days postoperatively.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Post-operative pain cores for the study group and the control group were 0.48 +/- 0.67 and 1.70 +/- 0.69 (P &lt; 0.001) at 1 day, 0.18 +/- 0.39 and 0.65 +/- 0.55 (P &lt; 0.05) at 4 days, respectively. No severe complications were found in either group.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">tramadol plays an important role in efficacy and safety for controlling ocular pain and inflammation after LASEK.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
